  {
    "episode_id": 75527,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75527.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "An abrupt pulling of a procedural vote in the US Senate on the Obamacare repeal and replace legislation means that bill will not make it through the Senate before the 4th of July recess. I don't think that was too much of a surprise this week when a number of senators, including Wisconsin's own Ron Johnson, came out strongly against the bill, indicating that they could and probably would support it with some tweaking. that the bare bones, the skeletal outline of this bill is palatable to them, but that it can't be rushed through because as Johnson put it on the J Weber show earlier this week, we've got one chance and we need to do it. Right. Does this jeopardize repeal and replace as at the Fourth of July recess, senators are going to go home and you had better believe that there will be a well organized effort to make sure that protesters meet them every step of the way. Will some of the squishier, more moderate Republicans go soft? And will the bill not be as palatable to conservatives like Johnson and Mike Lee and Ted Cruz as it's assumed that it might be joining me now to talk about the state of Obamacare, repeal and replace and the buzz in Washington for the last couple of days before that Fourth of July recess is a great friend of the show, Wisconsin Congressman Sean Duffy. Sir, how you doing?",
